<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
        xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
        xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
        xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
        http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-latest.xsd">

    <!--
        CONVERGENCE of prg_report's type column across the two states dcre_pay can be in.

        THE DEFECT. PRG crashed on kind-dcre-dev (measured 2026-09-11) with
        'column "prg_report.type" does not exist'. The live table is a schema generation
        from BEFORE the v1 rename and carries CRG's spelling:

          dcre_pay.prg_report  id, client, report_type, trigger_kind, window_key,
                               parent_source_msg_id, file_name, created_at, job_name

        Everything in this repository says `type`: the createTable in
        2026/08/002-pay-reporting.xml, PrgReportEntity.type, and the tests. The rename to
        `type` is deliberate and documented (a column never repeats its own table name), so
        the code is right and the DATABASE is behind.

        WHY NOTHING REPORTED IT. prg-002-report gained a re-run guard,
        <not><tableExists tableName="prg_report"/></not> onFail="MARK_RAN". On the stale
        database the table is present, so the guard fails, MARK_RAN records the createTable
        as applied, and the migration reports SUCCESS over a table whose shape it never
        checked. The drift then became permanent and silent: a green migration, a green
        suite, and a service that cannot write a report row. A tableExists guard asserts a
        NAME; it says nothing about COLUMNS, and it is the mechanism by which a stale schema
        gets certified as current.

        WHY A RENAME AND NOT A NEW createTable. The rows already in prg_report are the
        registry of reports already emitted, and prg_report.file_name is the restart no-op
        lookup (R-24). Dropping and recreating the table would make every previously emitted
        window look unemitted and re-issue it. renameColumn preserves the rows.

        HOW THE TWO STATES CONVERGE. The precondition tests for the OLD column rather than
        guarding against the new one, so it reads the actual shape rather than a name:

          - STALE database (column is report_type): the precondition PASSES, the rename runs,
            and the column becomes `type`. Converged.
          - FRESH database (2026/08 created the column as `type`): report_type is absent, the
            precondition FAILS, MARK_RAN records the changeset without executing it, and the
            column stays `type`. Converged, and the history row means no later run re-asks.

        MARK_RAN is the right failure mode here specifically because the skip it records
        permanently is a skip of work that is permanently unnecessary: on a database that was
        born correct there is no future moment at which this rename becomes wanted. That is
        the opposite of the A-79 / A-81 hazard, where MARK_RAN recorded a skip against a
        database that was merely not yet ready.

        Verified on this cluster: CockroachDB accepts ALTER TABLE x RENAME COLUMN a TO b.
    -->
    <changeSet id="prg-v2-report-type-rename" author="dcre">
        <preConditions onFail="MARK_RAN">
            <columnExists tableName="prg_report" columnName="report_type"/>
        </preConditions>
        <renameColumn tableName="prg_report" oldColumnName="report_type" newColumnName="type"/>
        <rollback>
            <renameColumn tableName="prg_report" oldColumnName="type" newColumnName="report_type"/>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
